.claude/
/node_modules/
tasks/
venv/

# Agent skills installed from upstream (vercel-labs/agent-skills et al.).
# Assistant tooling, not application code — same call as .claude/ and
# tasks/ above. skills-lock.json pins them by hash, so anyone who wants
# the same set reinstalls rather than inheriting a checked-in copy.
.agents/
skills-lock.json

# Working notes and the running build list. Same call as tasks/ above:
# planning docs churn faster than the code and aren't the record of what
# shipped — the migrations, tests and commit messages are.
build-list.md
next.md
full-audit-fixes.md

# graphify knowledge graph: 4.5MB of generated output (graph.json,
# graph.html, GRAPH_REPORT.md) rebuilt from source by `graphify update .`.
graphify-out/

# Playwright MCP debug artifacts (console logs + page snapshots)
.playwright-mcp/

# VS Code Counter: timestamped line-count reports, one directory per run.
# Generated output about the code rather than code, and it accumulates a
# new directory every time the extension is run.
.VSCodeCounter/

# Supabase: track migrations + tests (the security model), ignore
# CLI-local and secret artifacts only.
supabase/.branches/
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.env

# Manual pre-migration pg_dump backups (schema + DATA — this repo is public
# and auth.* tables carry live refresh tokens, not just PII). Local safety
# net only; never belongs in git history.
pre-*-data.sql
pre-*-schema.sql
# Design reference library (VoltAgent/awesome-design-md), read-only, not ours to track
/design-md/

# Screen recordings used as build specs (the two 2026-08-28 prototype walkthroughs).
# ~40MB each — reference material, not source, and git has no good answer for
# binaries this size. Keep them locally; they are not the record of what shipped.
*.mov
.vercel

# Every env file, at any depth. The negation matters: without it `.env*` also
# swallows the template, and a NEW .env.example added later would silently
# never be tracked — frontend/.env.example survives today only because it was
# committed before this rule existed. Verified with `git check-ignore -v`.
.env*
!.env.example
!*.env.example

# Personal ops reference: real resource IDs, IPs, account names. None of it
# is a secret by itself, but none of it needs to sit in a public repo's
# history either. The scripts that do the actual work are tracked under
# infra/ and server/ — this is just the narrative of where things ended up.
production-runbook.md
domain-change.md

# `.local.` names a file as a personal/dev-only artifact by convention
# (mirrors .env.local). scripts/seed-ux.local.mjs hardcodes Supabase's
# public local-dev demo keys, harmless only against a local Docker stack —
# this stops a future edit that points it at a real project from shipping
# by accident.
*.local.mjs
*.local.js
*.local.ts

# Same convention, for SQL. supabase/snippets/reset_to_admin_only.local.sql
# is the destructive prod clear-down with the real admin address filled in;
# the tracked reset_to_admin_only.sql keeps REDACTED-ADMIN-EMAIL. This repo
# is public, so a real address must never reach its history — and a wipe
# script that already names the one account to spare is a loaded gun to
# leave lying in a public repo besides.
*.local.sql

azure-clerk-migration-playbook.md